5. TWO OPEN LICENSING QUESTIONS, RECORDED HONESTLY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  (i) THE ONE-PATCH GAP. The published `legacy` branch declares version
      **0.7.0**. This port was derived from **0.7.1** — the newest 1.6.4 build
      iChun released, and the one the 2014 modpack shipped — via a decompile
      (48 `.java` files, against the branch's 41). The overwhelming majority
      of what was derived (the identity model, the twelve abilities, the
      transition rules, the command tree, the config defaults, player morphs)
      is present in the LGPL-3.0 0.7.0 source and is therefore squarely
      covered. A strict reader could nonetheless argue that whatever changed
      between 0.7.0 and 0.7.1 was never offered under any licence. That is the
      whole of the exposure, and it is small.

      Nothing iChun publishes contradicts the LGPL-3.0 reading. The iChunUtil
      end-user licence agreement (last updated 2019-03-04) is a
      warranty-and-where-to-download disclaimer: it addresses liability,
      third-party download sites and redistribution accountability, and says
      nothing at all about derivative works, modification, or source reuse.
      iChun's Morph page states no licence and links to the GitHub source as a
      contribution target.

 (ii) THE "SOFTWARE LIBRARY" QUESTION. The LGPL is drafted around libraries. A
      Minecraft mod is not obviously a library. The usual reading in the
      modding community is that a mod jar qualifies, because it is loaded by,
      and links against, other code. If you disagree, the LGPL-3.0 expressly
      permits you to treat your copy of this work as being under the GNU GPL
      version 3 instead; you are welcome to do so.

Neither question affects your rights as a recipient in any way that costs you
something: under LGPL-3.0 (or GPL-3.0, at your option) you may use, study,
